.TH SYS_LOG 2
.SH NAME
sys_log \- read the console's log (SYS_LOG)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_log(void *buf, size_t size, uint64_t *pos);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_log
copies the console's log into
.IR buf :
what a serial wire shows, the kernel's messages and the console server's
output (which goes through
.BR sys_debug (2)),
from byte
.I *pos
since boot, and advances
.IR *pos .
The kernel keeps the last
.B LOG_KEEP
(32 KiB) bytes; a position older than that is moved up to the oldest
kept. At most 1024 bytes come per call: call again until it returns 0.
.PP
Anyone may read it: it is what the serial wire shows.
.BR dmesg (1)
prints it.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes copied (0: nothing new), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I buf
or
.I pos
is not the caller's memory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dmesg (1),
.BR sys_debug (2),
.BR con (4)
